<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lomé</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lomé is the capital and chief port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> made it the capital of the colony of Togoland in 1897 and built a wharf and railways inland. It remained the capital of French Togoland and of independent Togo from 1960, and gave its name to the Lomé Conventions between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and African, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and Pacific states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lome.jpg" alt="Lomé" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reich geck · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%E2%80%99est_Lom%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lomé lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> at the south-western corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, with its western edge along the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, a few hundred metres from the city centre. Its name comes from Alomé, which in the Ewe language means &quot;in the alo trees&quot;, a reference to the forest that stood there. Despite its latitude near the equator it has a tropical savanna climate, because it lies in the Dahomey Gap, with annual rainfall of 800 to 900 millimetres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was founded by the Ewes and grew in the 19th century through German, British and African traders. British customs duties on alcohol and tobacco weighed heavily in the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, and Ewe traders from the Aflao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keta">Keta</a> area chose the nearby coast at Lomé to unload goods out of reach of customs officers. Growth began around 1880, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> caravans from the cola routes joined European firms there. Togoland was created within the German colonial empire on 5 July 1884 by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togoville">Togoville</a>, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-nachtigal">Gustav Nachtigal</a> and King Mlapa III.</p><p>A road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kpalime">Kpalimé</a> was begun in 1892. A port opened in 1904 and made Lomé the only maritime contact point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, ruining its rival Aného, and railways reached Aného in 1905, Kpalimé in 1907 and Atakpamé in 1909.</p><p>The population was 8,000 in 1914. German discriminatory patents and licences gradually pushed African traders out of import and export. In 1916 German companies were evicted and replaced by British and French firms. The French authorities created a council of notables in 1922, elective from 1925. In January 1923 a Lomé Women's Revolt forced the release of two arrested Duawo leaders, and a tax increase provoked riots in January 1933. The population grew from 30,000 in 1950 to 80,000 in 1960, the year of independence, and 200,000 in 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lomé is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, with an urban population of 837,437 and 2,188,376 residents in its metropolitan area at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its port, with a free zone opened in 1968, handles much of the transit trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>. The Lomé Convention between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and African, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and Pacific countries was first signed there on 28 February 1975, and the Lomé Peace Accord for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> on 7 July 1999. The University of Lomé was founded in 1970. Colonial buildings include the Sacred Heart Cathedral in German neo-Gothic style, and the Akodesséwa district holds a fetish market.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lomé” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In September 1999 African leaders resolved at Sirte in Libya to replace the OAU, and at <b>Lomé</b> in 2000 they adopted the Constitutive Act of the African Union.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Allied conquest of German Togoland in 1914, France took the larger eastern part, including the capital <b>Lome</b> and the railways, in the division of 1916.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-togoland">French Togoland</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital moved to <b>Lome</b> in 1897.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-togoland">German Togoland</a></li></ul></section>
